ree to explore by all  nations and that no party may lay claim  to any region of space or celestial body  this is all well and good but the  problem with the outer space treaty is  that it was drafted back in the 1960s  when our understanding of space was  considerably more limited than it is  today which means much of the language  was left fairly ambiguous fast forward  to more recent years and we've learned  that asteroids are often full of  incredibly valuable resources and that  we might be able to mine them there are  even some reports to suggest a  particular asteroid could be worth  upwards of 20 trillion dollars that's  one way to get the attention of large  corporations and eccentric billionaires  like Elon Musk and Jeff Bezos and  conveniently the Outer Space Treaty  doesn't explicitly say you can't extract  celestial resources as long as you don't  claim the area from which you're  extracting them but just to be safe the  u.s. passed the Space Act of 2015 which  clarified the country's position on  their rights in outer space the law  explicitly allows US citizens and  corporations to engage in the commercial  exploration and exploitation of space  resources including water and minerals  as some of the world's richest  individuals suit up to build their own  space resource extraction operations we  should look back to a forgotten episode  of the early days of space exploration  back in the 1960s a group of  underdeveloped nations banded together  to form a coalition known as the group  of 77 this group used the strength of  their numbers among UN nations to try to  influence international space law to  prevent it from disproportionately  benefiting the world's wealthiest  countries their goal was a more  equitable distribution of profits  derived from outer space resources they  believed that space was the common  heritage of all humankind  and as such should be utilized to  benefit the entire human population and  to help fund underdeveloped nations  rather than let extraordinary wealth  pile up in the hands of a few powerful  countries the group of 77 understood the  language of the outer space treaty to  call for a new economic order that would  reduce global poverty and increase the  well-being of all nations the treaty  says the exploration and use of outer  space shall be carried out for the  benefit and in the interest of all  countries irrespective of their degree  of economic or scientific development  and shall be the province of all mankind  in 1971 delegate to the Committee on the  peaceful uses of outer space proposed a  new legal designation for the final  frontier that of the common heritage of  mankind this was a fairly new idea  originally applied to maritime law just  a few years earlier if space were given  this designation it would lend legal  support for the Democratic distribution  of revenues derived from outer space in  1973 the group officially called for an  amendment to the Outer Space Treaty to  include the common heritage language one  delegate neatly summed up the group's  position saying it does not seem  justifiable that space activities should  evolve in a climate of total  laissez-faire which would conceal under  the cloak of rationality new ways for an  abusive exercise of power by those who  exert control over technology  despite pushback from both the US and  the Soviet Union not to mention a horde  of angry businessmen the final version  of the draft did include the common  heritage language  1979 when the amendment came up for  ratification it was met with intense  opposition including pushback from the  Reagan administration and eventually the  group had to abandon it another group  made a similar attempt in 1988 and was  also forced into submission by 1996 the  United States had clarified their stance  on the benefit clause ii mean vague  assurances of helping underdeveloped  nations build their own space technology  the final word from the UN outer space  committee came in 